kubectl Commands and Practice

1. Introduction

kubectl is the command-line tool used to communicate with a Kubernetes cluster.

During my internship learning, I practiced basic kubectl commands to create, view, manage, and troubleshoot Kubernetes resources.

2. Checking Kubernetes Version

The following command can be used to check the kubectl client version:

kubectl version --client

3. Checking Cluster Information

The following command displays basic information about the Kubernetes cluster:

kubectl cluster-info

4. Viewing Nodes

To view the nodes available in the cluster:

kubectl get nodes

Detailed information about a node can be viewed using:

kubectl describe node <node-name>

5. Viewing Pods

To view Pods:

kubectl get pods

To view Pods with additional information:

kubectl get pods -o wide

6. Viewing Deployments

Deployments can be viewed using:

kubectl get deployments

Detailed information can be viewed using:

kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name>

7. Viewing Services

Services can be viewed using:

kubectl get services

A shorter form of the command is:

kubectl get svc

8. Creating Resources Using YAML

Kubernetes resources can be created or updated from a YAML file using:

kubectl apply -f <file-name>.yaml

For example:

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

9. Deleting Resources

A Kubernetes resource can be deleted using:

kubectl delete -f <file-name>.yaml

For example:

kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml

10. Viewing Pod Logs

Logs generated by a Pod can be viewed using:

kubectl logs <pod-name>

This is useful for checking application output and basic troubleshooting.

11. Describing Resources

The `kubectl describe` command provides detailed information about a Kubernetes resource.

Example:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

It can help in understanding the current state of the resource and identifying basic issues.

12. Basic Command Summary

Command | Purpose

kubectl get pods | View Pods

kubectl get nodes | View nodes

kubectl get deployments | View Deployments

kubectl get services | View Services

kubectl apply -f | Create or update a resource

kubectl delete -f | Delete a resource

kubectl describe | View detailed resource information

kubectl logs | View Pod logs

kubectl cluster-info | View cluster information

13. Basic Kubernetes Practice Workflow

A basic workflow can be:

Create YAML file
       ↓
kubectl apply
       ↓
Create Kubernetes resource
       ↓
kubectl get
       ↓
Check resource status
       ↓
kubectl describe / logs
       ↓
Check and troubleshoot
       ↓
kubectl delete

14. Learning Outcome

Through this practice, I became familiar with the kubectl command-line tool and learned how to view, create, update, inspect, and delete basic Kubernetes resources.

I also gained practical understanding of using kubectl with Pods, Deployments, Services, and YAML configuration files.
